-*   Not given in the sources.
-
-### Strengths and Limitations
-*   **Strengths:**
-    *   Captures artistic lighting styles effectively [S1][S2, unofficial].
-    *   View-dependent lighting effects appear regular and rotate with the camera [S1][S2, unofficial].
-    *   Images of illuminated spheres are relatively easy to obtain [S1][S2, unofficial].
-    *   Extraction process allows building a large database of styles with little effort [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:**
-    *   Fails to capture complex aspects of realistic illumination [S1][S2, unofficial].
-    *   Does not distinguish between front and back faces [S1][S2, unofficial].
-
-### When to Use
-*   Use when the goal is to represent the general shading style of an object in an illustrative or non-photorealistic manner [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   Use when view-dependent lighting is desired without complex light transport calculations [S1][S2, unofficial].
-
-### When Not to Use
-*   Do not use when realistic illumination is required [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   Do not use when front/back face shading distinction is critical [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per fragment for the lookup, assuming constant-time texture sampling [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(R^2)$ for the lit sphere map texture, where $R$ is the resolution [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Well-suited to represent the general shading style of an object; images are relatively easy to obtain; allows for a large database of styles with little effort; view-dependent lighting effects appear regular [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Fails to capture complex aspects of realistic illumination [S1][S2, unofficial]. Cannot distinguish front/back faces, which may be undesirable in some surface-based contexts but is accepted for volume gradients [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When a stylized, artistic lighting appearance is desired that moves with the camera, and computational efficiency is prioritized over physical accuracy [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When physically accurate light transport or complex shadowing/occlusion effects are required [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-
-### Origin
-*   The method was presented by Sloan et al. in [SMGG01] for non-photorealistic rendering of polygonal models [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   It was adapted for volume rendering in "Style Transfer Functions for Illustrative Volume Rendering" [S1][S2, unofficial].
-
-### History and Development
-*   Sloan et al. described a method for extracting lit sphere maps from non-spherical regions in artwork, providing an interactive tool for rapid extraction [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   The technique is well-suited for illustrative volume rendering due to its simplicity and ability to capture artistic styles [S1][S2, unofficial].
+Lit sphere shading was originally presented by Sloan et al. [SMGG01] for non-photorealistic rendering of polygonal models [S1][S2, unofficial]. It was adapted for illustrative volume rendering by [Style Transfer Functions for Illustrative Volume Rendering](S1, S2). The technique leverages the fact that a sphere under orthographic projection covers the complete set of unit normals on one hemisphere, making it an ideal proxy for capturing normal-dependent shading variations [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
